  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Question": "When representing a vector with a directed line segment, what must be specified?",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "Only the length",
    "B": "Only the starting point",
    "C": "Only the line containing it",
    "D": "Starting point, endpoint, and direction"
  },
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "Length alone does not determine a nonzero vector. For distinct points \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\), a directed segment \\(\\overrightarrow{PQ}\\) starts at \\(P\\), ends at \\(Q\\), and points from \\(P\\) to \\(Q\\); its length gives the vector's magnitude. The ordered endpoints already determine its direction, so D lists that information redundantly.\n\nTranslating the entire segment preserves its length and direction, so the same vector can have representatives with different starting points. If \\(P=Q\\), the segment represents the zero vector, whose magnitude is \\(0\\) and whose direction is indeterminate. With this zero-vector exception, D is the intended choice for specifying a directed-segment representative.",
